steps.body:
Square brackets build a list of values, like `[3, 4]`. You will meet these lists properly in the Arrays lesson. For now, use them as a pattern: put a list on the right and a list of names on the left, and JavaScript unpacks the values into the names in order. This is called **array destructuring**.

```javascript
const [x, y] = [3, 4];
console.log(x, y); // 3 4
```

A name with no matching value gets `undefined`.

The same pattern swaps variables without a helper. The right side builds a temporary list from the current values, then the left side unpacks it:

```javascript
let a = 1, b = 2;
[a, b] = [b, a];
console.log(a, b); // 2 1
```

The classic way uses a temporary variable. It works in most languages and is just as correct:

```javascript harness
let a = 1, b = 2;
{{code}}
console.log(a, b);
```

```javascript
const temp = a;
a = b;
b = temp;
```

**Gotcha:** a statement that starts with `[` or `(` joins the line before it when that line has no semicolon. So `console.log(a)` on one line followed by `[a, b] = [b, a];` on the next is read as `console.log(a)[a, b] = [b, a];` and throws a TypeError. Ending every statement with a semicolon prevents this.

example.code:
const [first, second] = ["gold", "silver"];
console.log(first, second);

let left = 1, right = 2;
console.log("before:", left, right);
[left, right] = [right, left];
console.log("after:", left, right);

